(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-drill-antifungals-antivirals"]={"title":"Antifungals & Antivirals Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antifungal causes visual disturbance in about thirty per cent of patients?","choices":["Voriconazole","Fluconazole","Itraconazole","Posaconazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Visual effects in 30% — the standout adverse effect of this azole. Used for systemic Aspergillus and Candida. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which antifungal's infusion causes fever and chills severe enough to limit treatment?","choices":["Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Caspofungin","Griseofulvin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fever and chills, hypotension, and hypokalaemia with hypomagnesaemia — side effects that limit treatment. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which is the only azole effective against the Zygomycetes, Mucor and Rhizopus?","choices":["Fluconazole","Voriconazole","Posaconazole","Itraconazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The ONLY azole covering Zygomycetes. It also prolongs the QT interval and drops potassium and magnesium. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which antifungal penetrates the central nervous system and is used for cryptococcal meningitis?","choices":["Amphotericin B","Griseofulvin","Caspofungin","Fluconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Best oral absorption of the azoles and it crosses into the central nervous system. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which antifungal is teratogenic?","choices":["Itraconazole","Fluconazole","Terbinafine","Nystatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Teratogenic — the flag attached to this azole specifically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which antifungal inhibits CYP3A4 and so carries significant drug interactions?","choices":["Griseofulvin","Ketoconazole","Nystatin","Caspofungin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A CYP3A4 inhibitor, which is why its interaction list is the longest of the azoles. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which antifungal induces CYP1A2 and CYP2C9 and is absorbed better with a high-fat meal?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Fluconazole","Griseofulvin","Terbinafine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An inducer, taken with a fatty meal, and it causes headache in about 15% with blurred vision. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which antifungal is combined with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis and is hepatotoxic?","choices":["Fluconazole","Voriconazole","Micafungin","Flucytosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Given with amphotericin B in cryptococcal meningitis; hepatotoxicity is its flag. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which class is used for oesophageal candidiasis and for aspergillosis that has failed itraconazole or amphotericin?","choices":["Echinocandins","Polyenes","Allylamines","Griseofulvin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin — with tachycardia, headache, insomnia and low potassium and magnesium. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal has NO activity against Candida?","choices":["Nystatin","Tolnaftate","Clotrimazole","Miconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tolnaftate has no candidal activity — nystatin is the one for candidal infection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which antiviral crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration must be maintained?","choices":["Oseltamivir","Ganciclovir","Acyclovir","Famciclovir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Crystallises in the renal tubule; it also causes seizures, delirium and tremor centrally. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which antiviral causes neutropenia in fifteen to forty per cent of patients?","choices":["Acyclovir","Oseltamivir","Valacyclovir","Ganciclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neutropenia in 15-40%, with central effects in 5-15%; a third of patients must stop intravenous treatment because of side effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which antiviral is used for influenza A and B and requires renal dose adjustment?","choices":["Oseltamivir","Acyclovir","Ganciclovir","Famciclovir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A neuraminidase inhibitor for influenza A and B, with renal dose adjustment. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"In chickenpox, giving an antiviral within the first twenty-four hours does what?","choices":["Cures the infection completely","Shortens the illness but does not cure it","Prevents post-herpetic neuralgia entirely","Has no effect on the course at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Within the first 24 hours it shortens the acute illness — and it does not clear the latent virus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which agents are used for hookworms, roundworms, pinworms and whipworms?","choices":["Pyrantel pamoate","Metronidazole","Albendazole","Ivermectin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The benzimidazoles, and they are very well tolerated. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which drug is used to eliminate nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus, especially MRSA?","choices":["Bacitracin","Neomycin","Polymyxin B","Mupirocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Applied intranasally to clear carriage, MRSA included. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic frequently causes sensitisation?","choices":["Neomycin","Mupirocin","Bacitracin","Clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Frequently sensitises, and it can accumulate systemically. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which topical agent must be kept off open or denuded skin in high doses?","choices":["Bacitracin","Polymyxin B","Mupirocin","Nystatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Avoided on open wounds or denuded skin at high dose, because of neurotoxicity and nephrotoxicity. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal comes as a nail lacquer for onychomycosis?","choices":["Nystatin","Tolnaftate","Ciclopirox","Clotrimazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The nail lacquer formulation, applied to the nail plate. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which topical class is first-line for superficial dermatophyte infection — athlete's foot, ringworm and jock itch?","choices":["Nystatin","Mupirocin","Bacitracin","Terbinafine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Terbinafine, oral or topical, for the superficial dermatophytes. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which agent is used for external and perianal warts, actinic keratoses and basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Imiquimod","Mupirocin","Ciclopirox","Tacrolimus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three indications, and it irritates the skin in virtually every patient — which is expected, not a reason to stop. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which topical agent is used for recurrent orolabial herpes simplex?","choices":["Imiquimod","Penciclovir","Mupirocin","Bacitracin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The topical antivirals for cold sores. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which antifungal class covers Candida, Cryptococcus, Blastomyces, Histoplasma, Coccidioides and Aspergillus?","choices":["Echinocandins","Allylamines","Azoles","Polyenes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The class spectrum — which is why the individual azoles are then separated by their own quirks. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which antifungal accumulates its cyclodextrin vehicle in renal failure?","choices":["Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Caspofungin","Voriconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cyclodextrin carrier accumulates when renal function is poor. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which drug is used with amphotericin B specifically because using it alone breeds resistance?","choices":["Flucytosine","Fluconazole","Voriconazole","Griseofulvin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Paired with amphotericin B in cryptococcal meningitis rather than used alone. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia during treatment point to which antifungal?","choices":["Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Terbinafine","Nystatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Electrolyte wasting is part of its toxicity, alongside the infusion fever and hypotension. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which antiviral pair has actions and toxicity essentially the same as acyclovir?","choices":["Ganciclovir","Oseltamivir","Famciclovir","Albendazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Explicitly described as similar to acyclovir in both action and toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Seizures, delirium and tremor are the central nervous system effects of which antiviral?","choices":["Oseltamivir","Albendazole","Imiquimod","Acyclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Central effects alongside the renal crystallisation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal is the one used for vulvovaginal candidiasis?","choices":["Clotrimazole","Tolnaftate","Terbinafine","Ciclopirox"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical and vaginal use for vulvovaginal candidiasis. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which antifungal is used for systemic aspergillosis and candidaemia and is given intravenously as an echinocandin?","choices":["Griseofulvin","Caspofungin","Nystatin","Terbinafine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An echinocandin, used when itraconazole or amphotericin has failed. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"}]};
